{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 47, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1105 until his death in 1152. He was the eldest son of Raymond II of Toulouse and his wife Elvira of Castile making him a member of the prestigious House of Toulouse. Raymond III was born around 1092 and inherited the title after his father Raymond II Count of Toulouse died in 1105.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n**Early life and succession to Tripoli**\nRaymond III was one of the youngest central figures in the early history of the Crusader States. He was sent to Tripoli by his father to be raised and educated at the court of Baldwin I of Jerusalem. Baldwin I who was Raymond II's brother-in-law married Raymond's aunt Adelaide of Toulouse. This connection allowed Raymond III to grow up in the royal court of Jerusalem and gained firsthand experience in the military and governance of the Crusader States. When he came of age Raymond III returned to Tripoli in 1105 taking control of the county after his father's death.\n\nRaymond III continued the work of his predecessors in expanding and securing the county of Tripoli. He faced several military challenges including conflicts with the neighboring Muslim states and internal struggles within the Crusader States. One of his early tasks was to secure the agreement of the inhabitants of Tripoli to his rule which he accomplished by marrying Almodis of Leuca a Byzantine noblewoman who provided him with strong local support.\n\n*Family and political alliances*\nRaymond III's strategic marriages played a significant role in his political career. His first wife Almodis of Leuca bore him several children and strengthened his Byzantine connections. However after her death Raymond III married Hodierna of Jerusalem the sister of Queen Melisende of Jerusalem. This marriage not only reinforced his ties with the Kingdom of Jerusalem but also elevated his status within the Crusader States. Through his second marriage Raymond III became the uncle of King Baldwin III of Jerusalem.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n**Military campaigns and contributions**\nRaymond III was an active military leader and contributed significantly to the defense and expansion of the Crusader States. In 1109 he participated in the Siege of Sidon which resulted in the city's capture by the Crusaders. This victory was crucial as it prevented the city from falling back into Muslim hands and ensured a valuable port remained under Christian control. In 1110 Raymond III faced the Seljuk Turks in the Battle of Ager Sanguinis also known as the Battle of the Field of Blood. Although the battle was a heavy defeat for the Crusaders Raymond III's leadership and resilience were notable.\n\nOne of his most notable achievements was the capture of the castle of La Roche in 1123. This victory was part of a concerted effort by the Crusader States to secure the northern borders and protect the County of Tripoli from infiltration by Muslim forces. Raymond III's military campaigns required careful planning and diplomacy involving alliances with other Crusader lords and sometimes even with Muslim rulers to ensure his objectives were met.\n\n*Highlighted section part 3*\n**Later years and death**\nIn his later years Raymond III focused on consolidating his rule and ensuring the stability of the County of Tripoli. He promoted economic development by encouraging trade and reconstruction efforts particularly after the devastation caused by earlier conflicts. Tripoli became an important maritime hub benefiting from Raymond III's policies and strategic location.\n\nRaymond III died in 1152 at the age of around 60 after an illness. He was succeeded by his son Raymond of Antioch who would eventually become Prince of Antioch. Raymond III's legacy endured as he left behind a stable and prosperous county that continued to play an important role in the Crusader States. His strategic marriages political acumen and military contributions cemented his place as one of the key figures in the history of the Crusades. His reign marked a period of growth and resilience for the County of Tripoli ensuring its survival and prosperity in a turbulent era.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.07479121536016464}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.0001674750237725675}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0035772635601460934}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -2.722111225128174}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -3.2181143760681152}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.0002712835557758808}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.02735143154859543}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.4360888600349426}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.24788305163383484}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.0003147821989841759}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.03361810743808746}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -2.6036317348480225}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.40021759271621704}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.2666999101638794}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.04538675397634506}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -0.020609749481081963}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.33659711480140686}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0378950834274292}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -6.12716976320371e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.0006548406090587378}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.0031461049802601337}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.002089223824441433}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -1.0993002653121948}, {"token": " He", "logprob": -0.1911768764257431}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.6248608231544495}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.5453757047653198}, {"token": " eldest", "logprob": -1.711828589439392}, {"token": " son", "logprob": -0.10270670056343079}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.022152476012706757}, {"token": " Raymond", "logprob": -0.3393000364303589}, {"token": " II", "logprob": -0.1848246455192566}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.3629237413406372}, {"token": " Toulouse", "logprob": -2.115382432937622}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.